# Candidate dependency boundary verification

The first submitted candidate (target revision 199) was withdrawn without human review after its isolated build exposed a stale nested-package dependency. Its earlier working-directory checkpoint passed 120 server and 101 browser tests, but did not establish correctness of isolated dependency preparation.

Captured workspace and local directory dependency declarations now determine package scopes recursively. Local packages link only to captured source, with package-name and containment validation. Broken dependency copies fail explicitly. Shared manifest-only discovery orders owned builds before their consumers, detects cycles and missing owners, and does not run third-party package build scripts.

Verification after the correction:

- Five build-planner tests passed, including a cold TypeScript declaration/consumer regression.
- Ten materialization and Preview tests passed; the final recursive-scope refinement also passed its two affected isolation tests.
- A fresh actual build of TheBroccoli, BtrTemplate and PersoTemplate passed in 184 seconds. All three source fingerprints remained unchanged.
- Independent engine, harness and UI reviews accepted the final dependency boundary. Source was then frozen.

The new submitted candidate's mandatory checks are the final full checkpoint. Their authoritative results appear in the review itself; the earlier 120/101 run is not presented as the final result after this correction.

This isolates captured source and local package ownership. Installed third-party dependencies are copied into isolation; this does not claim a fresh lockfile-verified dependency installation. No human approval, source-repository commit or merge has occurred.
